WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.559
Julia had no idea what was happening. She couldn't hear anything but the rush of air around her.

2
00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.581
Then she landed on a hard marble floor.

3
00:00:13.582 --> 00:00:17.622
She stumbled forward, letting go of Brody’s arm.

4
00:00:17.623 --> 00:00:22.478
"What’s going on?" Brody shouted. "Hey!"

5
00:00:22.479 --> 00:00:26.563
Julia looked around, too frightened to speak.

6
00:00:26.564 --> 00:00:29.976
They were in a square room with stone walls.

7
00:00:29.977 --> 00:00:37.031
A skylight overhead let in a beam of bright sunshine.

8
00:00:37.032 --> 00:00:39.435
Two young men stood in front of them.

9
00:00:39.436 --> 00:00:44.379
Both were tall and handsome and dressed in short robes.

10
00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:51.757
"Chitons," Julia thought. She remembered learning that word in social studies class.

11
00:00:51.758 --> 00:00:57.482
Chitons were simple garments worn by men and women in the ancient world.

12
00:00:57.483 --> 00:01:03.961
The classes about ancient Greece and Rome had been among Julia’s favorite parts of social studies last year.

13
00:01:03.962 --> 00:01:06.736
But why were these guys wearing chitons?

14
00:01:06.737 --> 00:01:09.410
What had happened to the high school?

15
00:01:09.411 --> 00:01:18.002
"Where did the girl come from?" one guy asked. "She wasn't part of the plan."

16
00:01:18.003 --> 00:01:24.141
The young man's face was so beautiful that it took Julia a moment to notice something odd.

17
00:01:24.142 --> 00:01:29.088
He had only a single lock of brown hair hanging over his forehead.

18
00:01:29.089 --> 00:01:32.398
The rest of his head was completely bald.

19
00:01:32.399 --> 00:01:39.215
The second young man had curly blond hair and was as handsome as a movie star.

20
00:01:39.216 --> 00:01:46.171
He leaned forward and poked Julia. "You, girl," he said. "What are you doing here?"

21
00:01:46.172 --> 00:01:51.738
"Ow!" Julia yanked her arm away. "Stop poking me."

22
00:01:51.739 --> 00:01:57.960
"What's going on?" Brody demanded again. "Where are we?"

23
00:01:57.961 --> 00:02:02.993
"Silence, mortal." The blond man frowned at his friend.

24
00:02:02.994 --> 00:02:06.745
"The girl was a mistake. Let's toss her back through."

25
00:02:06.746 --> 00:02:11.094
"Are you certain you want to do that?" the bald guy said.

26
00:02:11.095 --> 00:02:18.138
"Remember, this passage will close forever as soon as anyone passes back through it."

27
00:02:18.139 --> 00:02:21.941
"I forgot about that." The blond guy looked annoyed.

28
00:02:21.942 --> 00:02:24.018
"In that case, she'll have to stay.

29
00:02:24.019 --> 00:02:27.402
We might need the passage again if this doesn't work out."

30
00:02:27.403 --> 00:02:32.336
He glanced at Brody and looked him up and down.

31
00:02:32.337 --> 00:02:39.372
"Are we being kidnapped?" Julia blurted out, still trying to figure out what was happening. "Who are you?"

32
00:02:39.373 --> 00:02:46.897
The blond guy chuckled. "Do not be frightened, mortal. We won't hurt you.

33
00:02:46.898 --> 00:02:53.630
Don't you recognize me? I am Hermes, messenger of the gods and friend to mortals."

34
00:02:53.631 --> 00:02:59.805
"Mortals? Gods?" Brody said. "Huh?"

35
00:02:59.806 --> 00:03:03.485
Hermes held up a hand to silence him.

36
00:03:03.486 --> 00:03:07.101
"And this is my friend Caerus, god of luck and opportunity.

37
00:03:07.102 --> 00:03:15.362
He saw the opportunity to help me humiliate Zeus by seeking a mortal champion in an unusual way."

38
00:03:15.363 --> 00:03:25.655
Hermes and Caerus both laughed heartily. Julia caught a glimpse of movement and looked down.

39
00:03:25.656 --> 00:03:30.531
Her eyes widened when she saw little golden wings on Hermes' sandals.

40
00:03:30.532 --> 00:03:34.795
They were flapping gently. Could it be . . . ?

41
00:03:34.796 --> 00:03:42.643
"I do have some influence over time and seasons," Caerus said, puffing out his chest proudly.

42
00:03:42.644 --> 00:03:47.695
"Not that Zeus would ever notice—he treats me like a

43
00:03:47.696 --> 00:03:52.611
demigod. He'll never figure out that I helped Hermes defeat him with a champion from the

44
00:03:52.612 --> 00:03:59.502
future." "What? This is stupid," Brody said loudly. "Look, I'm tired of your dumb

45
00:03:59.503 --> 00:04:03.421
stories. Why did you really grab me and drag me in

46
00:04:03.422 --> 00:04:07.746
here?" "It's quite simple, my simple mortal friend," Hermes

47
00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:12.071
said. "You see, the next Olympic Games will begin

48
00:04:12.072 --> 00:04:15.242
soon." "The Olympics?" Julia

49
00:04:15.243 --> 00:04:24.796
echoed. Caerus nodded. "And as usual, Zeus is already bragging about how his champion will easily defeat all

50
00:04:24.797 --> 00:04:31.245
others." "Yes." Hermes snorted. "He's being quite

51
00:04:31.246 --> 00:04:37.301
annoying. Just because he's king of the gods, he thinks nobody else can ever beat his hand-picked

52
00:04:37.302 --> 00:04:43.745
champion." Brody looked confused. "Zeus?" he muttered. "Wait, that sounds

53
00:04:43.746 --> 00:04:47.935
familiar. Is he from a video game or

54
00:04:47.936 --> 00:04:53.660
something?" "No way," Julia said, staring at the two men. "You're not

55
00:04:53.661 --> 00:04:56.810
real. None of this is real. Hermes is just a

56
00:04:56.811 --> 00:05:01.433
myth. So is Zeus—all the ancient Greek gods are from

57
00:05:01.434 --> 00:05:07.938
myths." "Greek gods? Like from social studies class?" Brody said with a

58
00:05:07.939 --> 00:05:13.063
snort. "Yeah, right. These two are probably just a couple of jerks from Southbridge

59
00:05:13.064 --> 00:05:16.425
High. They're trying to scare me before next week's

60
00:05:16.426 --> 00:05:21.708
game." Hermes traded a look with Caerus. "What are they blathering on

61
00:05:21.709 --> 00:05:30.031
about?" "Who cares," Caerus answered. He sounded bored again. "Let's move this along. I'm

62
00:05:30.032 --> 00:05:40.482
hungry." "All right." Hermes gestured to Brody. "Come, mortal. I've arranged for you to stay with a local family." He glanced at

63
00:05:40.483 --> 00:05:44.167
Julia. "I'm sure they can make room for the girl as

64
00:05:44.168 --> 00:05:53.671
well." "No." Julia's logical mind still refused to accept what Hermes and Caerus—or whatever their names might be—were telling her.

65
00:05:53.672 --> 00:06:00.895
"This isn't happening," she insisted. "We're not really in some temple in ancient Athens."

66
00:06:00.896 --> 00:06:07.024
Hermes sounded impatient. "Aren't you listening to a word I say, mortal girl?"

67
00:06:07.025 --> 00:06:12.391
"'Julia,'" she said. "That's my name, not 'mortal girl.'"

68
00:06:12.392 --> 00:06:17.446
Hermes ignored her. "Of course we're not in Athens. We're in Olympia.

69
00:06:17.447 --> 00:06:20.494
If you don't believe me, I'll prove it to you."

70
00:06:20.495 --> 00:06:28.569
He grabbed Julia's arm. Julia tried to shake him off, but his grip was as strong as iron.

71
00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:36.979
"Stop it," she yelled as he dragged her outside. "Let me go or I'll—whoa!"

72
00:06:36.980 --> 00:06:42.538
She stared in amazement at the sun-drenched scene spread out before her.

73
00:06:42.539 --> 00:06:51.123
It looked nothing like her hometown. It looked nothing like anywhere she'd ever seen, except in the pages of her social studies textbook.

74
00:06:51.124 --> 00:07:02.535
Large, gleaming marble buildings lined with columns dotted the landscape, along with many smaller structures. A mountain rose in the background.

75
00:07:02.536 --> 00:07:06.759
The only roads she could see were dirt ones, and there were no cars on them,

76
00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:13.258
just people in chitons walking or riding donkeys or driving carts pulled by oxen or horses.

77
00:07:13.259 --> 00:07:18.987
There were no power lines or billboards or tall buildings anywhere in sight.

78
00:07:18.988 --> 00:07:31.237
Julia's mind whirled with confusion as she realized what it looked like—an ancient Greek city!

